Looking at this scenario, the central issue is procedural legitimacy: a "consensus" achieved by excluding the very people affected by a decision is definitionally suspect, since consensus implies incorporation of all relevant perspectives, not just the absence of formal dissent. This makes option A in both questions weak—it treats procedural form as sufficient regardless of whether the substance of inclusion was honored.

At the same time, there's real value in not treating every contested outcome as grounds for external override of communal processes. The distinction between B and C is subtle: B focuses on the illegitimacy of the outcome itself, while C focuses on the appropriate remedy—fixing the process rather than simply declaring the council's authority void. Both are largely compatible; C could be read as a practical extension of B's diagnosis. The women's challenge is best understood not as rejecting communal decision-making as such, but as invoking its own internal standards (genuine consensus) to demand correction. This suggests weight should concentrate on B and C, with C slightly favored for offering a constructive path.

