Authorship norms (and basic fairness) hinge on distinguishing "contributed to the intellectual content of this specific work" from "was part of the broader environment that made the work possible." The team's foundation matters, but foundational/environmental support isn't the same as substantive contribution to *this* idea. So the researcher should credit people who actually shaped this paper's ideas, methods, or execution — not the whole team by default, and not herself alone if others did contribute meaningfully. Pure solo credit risks erasing real intellectual debts if others helped shape the idea; pure team credit risks diluting credit and mischaracterizing casual proximity as authorship. The most defensible position traces actual contribution, while still recognizing that individual insight arises within relationships that deserve acknowledgment (even if not authorship, e.g., via acknowledgments).

For the reasoning question, all three considerations have some truth: contributions are relationally embedded (A), individual effort deserves protection from being submerged into the collective (B), but the actionable fair principle is to trace actual causal/intellectual contribution (C), which implicitly incorporates the insights of A and B without collapsing into either extreme.

