The case pits legitimate patient-centered clinical judgment against the epistemic value of consensus built from broader, more rigorous evidence. Two successful cases are weak evidence — subject to selection effects, lack of controls, and confirmation bias — so treating them as decisive would be risky, especially against unanimous informed disagreement. At the same time, consensus is not infallible; standard protocols lag behind emerging practice, and doctors do have some latitude for innovation, especially in cases where standard treatment has failed. The strongest path preserves accountability while not foreclosing patient benefit: formal review, documentation, perhaps ethics consultation or informed consent processes for off-label treatment. This respects both the legitimacy of her clinical intuition and the systemic value of oversight, without either blind deference or unilateral action based on thin evidence.

For the second question, all three considerations are real: consensus exists to guard against individual bias (a real epistemic hazard, especially with only two data points); the doctor does have a duty to the specific patient, which matters ethically and is not fully captured by population-level guidelines; and there are institutional processes for handling exactly this kind of disagreement, which is what actually resolves the tension without asking her to be either a rule-follower or a rogue actor.

